o straight into our studio  connect where  we run any r script our script we load  any model execute the model  and then store the outputs in bigquery  and buckets in gcs  so as you can see here all of the code  required is just  in r so we can have interactions with  bigquery or the buckets  with using the relevant packages in r  so it's really nice to maintain really  easy for someone that knows  how to code in order to get up to speed  with and  we're quite happy with it until up to  now  so if we want to compare the two  pipelines  and so we said that now we only have r  whereas in the past we had three  different  languages so that's definitely a benefit  we removed a lot of the complexity  involved which was  not really necessary as it seems  we can now publish and schedule things  in just a few  minutes whereas in the past it would  take around a day  and if you were a new joiner maybe a  week  we can make the most out of shiny  dashboards and shiny apps and just  deploy anything we want  uh really easy uh  and also there is great integration to  our team's capabilities as i said we're  not software developers so  maintaining a pipeline that involves  docker and luigi  and interacting with aws was much much  harder than what we have now  and as you will see also in the end  the current pipeline fits greatly with  all of the agile processes we have  so now we can briefly go through what  setting up our studio connect  involves so as i said we are using  google but it would be more or less the  same process if you are using amazon or  any other  cloud provider so for us  it was just a matter of going to the  console create a new instance  which can be a very small linux server  in the beginning and then you can  directly ssh onto the server install of  all of the basic requirements like a  version of r  a version of java and r studio connect  itself  you can also create a server-wide data  store if you want to  allow for data to be loaded between  different jobs or  different different runs of the same job  and finally you just need to configure  packets and bigquery  create the relevant credentials and then  use those inside their studio connect  and that's it so in in the beginning it  might have taken us  a couple of days to get everything  configured  and create the proper documentation if  we were to replicate the server now  it would probably take just a couple of  hours  so finally moving back to our  sprint cycle and how our studio connect  and the new pipeline have  made us better working in an agile  setting so creating markdown reports  and providing results from any early  analysis we do is really easy now we can  move between  research and development to deployment  whenever we need to  we can deploy multiple versions of the  same model  again that's really really easy and on  top of that we can create  dashboards or alerts to make sure that  all of the models that we have  look fine and there is no performance  drop or  any failure in the scheduled runs  so then as an overview uh we  went quickly through those so we saw how  our studio connect is now part of our  pipeline and how it has improved  the way we do data science we briefly  described how  we configured and set up  our new pipeline using google  infrastructure  and we saw how  the deploying and scheduling markdowns  and dashboards have  made us better in working in an agile  setting  and finally how this also allows us to  make sure that stakeholders are engaged  and we  can welcome feedback throughout the  sprint cycle  so that was it that was it for me  just a reminder we're also hiring as i  said we're trying to double in size  i'm not sure how relevant this is  because most positions are going to be  in london but  feel free to follow the link and contact  either the team or me directly  thank you very much  so we have a a couple questions that  came through the first one is  um what parts of the pipeline are not  replaced with our studio connect  are not replaced by our studio connect  uh all of the our logic and the  interactions  uh with the database or the buckets  or how we execute models so it's yeah  the things that were replaced that might  be easier to answer  so we replaced jenkins and the  monitoring  and the dashboarding with all of the  capabilities of us to do connect  another question from glenn uh how do  you handle shiny dashboards that require  querying large amounts of data  that take considerable time  uh so we do use uh we do cast  the data so if there has been a more  recent run within the same day or within  the same  whatever period we want to update the  dashboard in  uh it's faster if it's not the first  time because we use the cast data  if it's the first one and the query time  is  quite long then the dashboard will take  quite a long tool  to load and uh one last question so is  there something intrinsic  to use rcd connect that motivated the  switch from s3  to the google cloud well gcs  no so so the move between amazon and  google was completely  coming from our data platforms team  because i think there are packages to  interact  both with aws or gc gcs so it's like  it's the same  we should have time for one one last one  so what type of challenges have you had  with scheduling jobs in connect  uh i don't think we had any issues with  scheduling  on a like time schedule we  one thing we would really really like to  have  is to have tricky trigger based  like runs of models so if we could have  some rules  under which the model would run instead  of just specifying that  the job needs to run every hour every  day to  have maybe another job to go in bigquery  check a field if the field had changed  then  run the model doesn't make sense thank  you  thank you

